yframe and just drag it to  wherever you want it to end because  these are roving keyframes we're too  close here to see any of the circles but  a quick solution of that is to add  another null object make it 3d parent  the camera to that Knoll and just move  that an old back in z space to create  the back movement and Z to simulate our  spiral move same with where the null  begins okay so we can see these circles  here  and I can also select both keyframes by  just selecting position and I can move  them just like this so I can focus my  scene war on what I want to see okay so  this solution worked the circles are off  the screen a little bit too much and  you'll notice too that we are doing the  opposite move we're going from a wide  part of the spiral to the narrow part of  the spiral and the easy way to fix that  if I want to do the opposite move is  just to select all keyframes go to keep  em assistant time reverse and now we'll  start tight and go wide as you can see  over here I can reposition  okay so here's this solution starts  really tight and goes really wide so  again this solution looks pretty cool as  well but when I did start this solution  I told you there's one of two ways you  could do this so this was the first way  with pasting the path right into the  position of the camera another way to do  this is to create a new shape layer  convert to a path so it's just a  straight up path and then paste the file  spiral from illustrator into the path of  the shape layer the advantage to doing  it this way is that I have control over  if I want the path to be smaller or  larger so it's just a little more  flexibility in the size of the path that  the camera will take because what I can  do now is size it to how I think it's  appropriate copy from here and then copy  it into the position of the camera so  you can see here it's much smaller path  if I think if that doesn't quite work I  can enlarge it maybe just say here copy  again and paste the path is sl